# Classname Token Workflow

Origin: generalized from a real className mining workflow of a
reference donor project — the classification contract and the AST/JSX
miner via the TypeScript Compiler API survived because they are tech-agnostic
within any React+Tailwind stack (or equivalent utility-CSS). What
was removed: the donor project's hardcoded paths, the donor project's
Postgres DSN, and the 13-table relational layer built specifically
for the data volume of that repo (see "Relational layer (optional,
advanced)" at the end — documented in prose, not ported as an executable
script; it is a candidate for a future rescue round if this project
also has data volume large enough to overflow the LLM context).

## When to use

Use when the task mentions className mining, repetition of
`className`/utility-class, class tokenization, DRY UI abstraction,
decentralized icons/sizes, or the risk of merging different entities
into the same token.

## Contract

1. Never promote textual repetition directly into a token.
2. Common utility classes (`flex items-center`, `gap-*`, `text-sm` and
   similar) are idioms of the utility-CSS framework until proven semantically
   otherwise.
3. The unit of analysis is the JSX/interactive entity: root, ancestors,
   siblings, props, route, roles, and icons.
4. Possible output types:
   - `style_token`: same visual, different entities.
   - `component_contract`: same visual/interactive entity.
   - `hook`: same behavior, different UI.
   - `registry`: shared enumeration/actions.
   - `no_abstraction`: textual savings without semantics.
5. Do not mix different product entities when side effects, routes,
   states, or operations diverge.

## Procedure

### 1. Run the miner

```bash
node .harness/lib/classname-miner-v2.mjs --root {{ harness_web_app_dir }}
```

Flags/env vars (all optional — `--help` lists the complete contract):

| Flag | Env var | Default | What it controls |
|------|---------|---------|-----------------|
| `--root <dir>` | `HARNESS_MINER_ROOT` | cwd | root scanned for `.ts`/`.tsx` |
| `--out <dir>` | `HARNESS_MINER_OUT_DIR` | `docs/design-system/sources` (relative to the git repo root) | where the `.md`/`.json` report is written |
| `--interactive-tags <csv>` | `HARNESS_MINER_INTERACTIVE_TAGS` | `a,button,Link` | extra tags treated as an interactive root (e.g., `Button,MenuItem` if your design-system uses those names) |
| `--emit-full <dir>` | — | — | full NDJSON dataset (non-truncated), for optional external ingestion |
| `--emit-json` | — | off | also writes the truncated JSON (archaeology/manual backup) |
| `--self-test` | — | off | validates the shape of the output schema |

The miner automatically resolves path-aliases (`@components/*`, `@/*`
etc.) by reading the `tsconfig.json`/`jsconfig.json` of the `--root` — there is no
hardcoded alias; a project without those files still works (fallback: without
alias resolution, relative resolution still works).

Zero-dependency variant (no `typescript` in `node_modules`, regex-only,
no structural JSX/cluster context): `node .harness/lib/classname-miner.mjs
--root {{ harness_web_app_dir }}` — same root/out flags; uses only text
n-grams, not role entities/clusters. Prefer the v2 above whenever
`typescript` is available — v1 is the fallback.

### 2. Read the report first

```bash
sed -n '1,180p' docs/design-system/sources/classname-token-mining-v2.md
```

The report has: top n-grams by raw savings, top role clusters,
high-signal clusters and (if applicable to the project) sidebar evidence —
each cluster already comes with `decision`, `confidenceScore`, `blockingReasons` and
`recommendedSplitBy` computed heuristically by the miner.

For large datasets (the full JSON can overflow the LLM context):
use `--emit-full <dir>` to generate NDJSON and query by line/grep instead
of loading everything — or see "Relational layer (optional, advanced)" below.

### 3. Classify the cluster before editing

- Reject if `antiMergeReasons` has `generic-structural-classes` and there is
  no semantic role.
- Review manually if there is `multiple-route-areas`,
  `multiple-interactive-tags` or `no-semantic-child-roles`.
- Propose a contract if `surfaceRole`, `roles`, `interactiveTag` and the
  samples point to the same entity.

### 4. Write the contract before migration

- name;
- single source;
- scope;
- anti-scope;
- visual invariants;
- proof routes/themes.

### 5. Migrate sequentially

One entity at a time. After each slice:

- `git diff --check`;
- typecheck or relevant test;
{%- if use_ui_evidence %}
- `cd {{ harness_web_app_dir }} && npm run ui:evidence -- after-<label> --routes <routes> --themes {{ harness_ui_evidence_themes }}`
  when there is a visual change (skill `ui-evidence` — full before/after protocol
  there).
{%- else %}
- before/after visual evidence capture, if this project has a renderable
  frontend (`use_ui_evidence` is off in this project — with no engine
  installed, visual proof is manual).
{%- endif %}

### 6. If you change the design system truth, update the Karpathy wiki

- `docs/design-system/wiki/*.md` for the current truth;
- `docs/design-system/index.md`;
- `docs/design-system/log.md`;
- `python3 .harness/lib/docs_wiki_lint.py --scope design-system`.

## Relational layer (optional, advanced)

If the miner dataset is too large for the LLM context even via
NDJSON, the origin pattern ingested the `--emit-full` into a dedicated
Postgres schema (13 tables: `entity`, `occurrence`, `semantic_cluster`,
`edge_cluster_entity` etc.) for relational querying instead of a file. This
was NOT ported as an executable script in this round — it is a candidate for
future rescue if this project has the same scale problem. If you choose to
recreate this pattern here: use a DSN from an environment variable of this
project's own (never hardcode host/port/credential from another project), and
treat the Postgres schema as a derivable cache — 100% regenerable from
`--emit-full`, never the source of truth.

## Final guardrail

A cluster is evidence, not authorization. Authorization comes from contract +
migration + rendered proof.
